---
description: Markdown widget — inline SQL, template expressions, styling
globs: dashboards/**/*.yaml
alwaysApply: false
---

<rule>
<meta>
  This rule covers the `markdown` widget: data-driven dynamic text with template expressions, formatting helpers, conditionals, and links.
  Activate when creating markdown widgets in dashboard YAML.
</meta>

<concepts>
  <engine>Eval-free template engine (no `new Function`, CSP-safe). Renders Markdown → HTML via `marked` + `DOMPurify`.</engine>
  <data_model>Inline SQL returns a single row. Each column becomes a template variable.</data_model>
  <notebook_markdown>In notebook mode, markdown widgets without datasource/template act as narrative cells and open in edit mode by default.</notebook_markdown>
  <doc_markdown>In `mode: doc` tabs, markdown widgets are the primary Knowledge Base body. Prefer static `content:` for durable prose; use `template:` + inline DuckDB `datasource:` only when the document needs calculated values. Static doc content is processed into a cached, rebuildable DocsArtifact: sanitized HTML, plain text, headings, ai_hints, wiki_links, Mermaid/future embed refs, warnings, and contentHash. In composite directory tabs, `mode: doc` belongs in `_tab.yaml`, not in tab-content section files.</doc_markdown>
</concepts>

<schema>
  <markdown_widget>
    ```yaml
    - id: summary
      type: markdown
      col_span: 6
      row_span: 2
      datasource:
        sql: |                              # Required. Inline SQL (DuckDB syntax)
          SELECT SUM(costs) AS total_costs, SUM(revenue_net) AS total_revenue
          FROM {{source:total_by_ad}}
      template: |                           # Required. Mustache-like template
        ## Total by Ad
        Revenue: **{{currency_short total_revenue}}**
        Costs: **{{currency_short total_costs}}**
      render:                               # Optional
        sanitize: true                      #   Sanitize HTML (default: true)
        allow_html: false                   #   Allow raw HTML in template
      style:                                # Optional
        prose: true                         #   Tailwind prose typography
        prose_size: base                    #   sm | base | lg
        custom_css: "border-b pb-4 mb-4"   #   Additional CSS classes
      links:                                # Optional
        enabled: true
        base_url: /dashboards/product/samples
    ```
  </markdown_widget>

  <notebook_markdown_cell>
    Static markdown cells can be used as narrative blocks in `mode: notebook` tabs and as durable prose in `mode: doc` Knowledge Base tabs:

    ```yaml
    - id: methodology
      type: markdown
      content: |
        ## Methodology
        Explain assumptions, formulas, and next steps here.
    ```

    Use `content:` for notebook narrative cells and doc pages. Use `template:` + inline `datasource:` when the markdown should render data-driven values.
  </notebook_markdown_cell>

  <doc_page_body>
    ```yaml
    mode: doc
    sections:
      - id: body
        widgets:
          - id: article
            type: markdown
            content: |
              ## Local Setup
              Write long-form internal knowledge here.
    ```

    Doc pages are normal dashboard tabs: source of truth is root YAML, canonical route is `/dashboards/<root>/<dashboard>/<tab>`, and RBAC uses the tab path. Do not introduce a separate docs store. The DocsArtifact cache is derived from YAML content and works with filesystem, MongoDB-backed, and in-memory roots.
    Simple tab files may put `mode: doc` next to `sections`; composite directory tabs must put `mode: doc` in `<tab>/_tab.yaml`, while `<tab>/01-main.yaml` contains only `title`, optional `datasources`, and `sections`.
  </doc_page_body>
</schema>

<doc_reader_extensions>
  <syntax>
    Reader extensions for static `mode: doc` content:

    | Syntax | Purpose |
    |---|---|
    | `[[target]]`, `[[target#anchor|label]]` | KB wiki links within the current page group; short links resolve target titles automatically and validation rejects broken links |
    | `<!--ai_hint: ...-->` | Hidden LLM/search context; stripped from reader HTML and plain text |
    | `:::note`, `:::warning`, `:::danger`, `:::tip` | Callouts; close each block with `:::` |
    | `:::details{title="..."}` | Collapsible explanation block |
    | `:::steps` | Procedure block for ordered steps |
    | `:::columns{count=2}` | Reader-only responsive columns for short parallel notes; `count=2` or `count=3` |
    | `:::tabs` + `::tab{title="..."}` | Accessible reader tabs for alternate explanations or code paths |
    | `:::embed{type=mermaid}` | Static client-rendered Mermaid diagram from the directive body; no data access |
    | `:::embed{type=widget id=...}` | Safe embed placeholder; live widget/metric/dashboard embeds are a follow-up |
    | `:kbd[Cmd+K]` | Keyboard shortcut chip |
    | `:status[verified]{color="green"}` | Inline status chip |
  </syntax>

  <authoring_rules>
    - Keep durable prose in static `content:` so the DocsArtifact can expose headings, plain text, links, and ai_hints to LLM/search consumers.
    - Do not put `mode` or `runtime` in tab-content files such as `01-main.yaml`; keep tab mode in the simple tab file or `_tab.yaml`.
    - Use meaningful h2/h3 headings; they populate the desktop TOC, mobile Contents sheet, and future retrieval anchors.
    - Good doc pages include a clear purpose, owner/review cadence, section-specific `<!--ai_hint: ...-->` comments, risks/caveats, procedural steps when relevant, and validated related reading.
    - Prefer `[[target]]` short links. Skipper displays the target page title automatically; use `[[target|custom label]]` only when the sentence needs different wording.
    - Link resolution is exact doc tab path/id first, then unambiguous leaf match with numeric filename prefixes ignored. Use explicit paths when a leaf could become ambiguous.
    - Use `[[target#heading-anchor]]` for deep links and keep anchors aligned with real headings. Validation fails unresolved links, ambiguous leaf matches, and missing anchors.
    - Keep dynamic numbers in DuckDB SQL (`template:` + inline `datasource:`) rather than hardcoding them in prose. Recompute ratios from components with `NULLIF` guards instead of pasting stale values.
    - Use Mermaid only for static process, dependency, or relationship diagrams. Do not encode live metrics in Mermaid; live values belong in DuckDB-backed widgets or markdown templates.
    - Use tabs for alternate views of the same concept, not for hiding required reading. Use columns for concise side-by-side notes; keep long prose in the normal single-column reader flow.
    - For static Knowledge Base pages, prefer markdown `content:` with no datasource-backed widgets. Static doc pages skip the browser DuckDB startup path and load faster; add inline DuckDB only when the page truly needs calculated values.
    - Do not add MDX, JSX, raw scripts, or iframe embeds; the reader sanitizes HTML and only reserves safe placeholders.
    - Do not introduce `kb_pages` or a separate docs route; filesystem, MongoDB-backed roots, and in-memory roots all use the same tab YAML source of truth.
  </authoring_rules>

  <reader_experience>
    - The KB reader uses a flat, opaque content column; Liquid Glass belongs to app chrome and the mobile TOC trigger, not the prose body.
    - The reader favors calm editorial scanning: bounded line length, generous line height, quiet metadata, strong heading rhythm, token-based callouts, accessible tabs, responsive columns, Mermaid diagrams, and copyable code blocks.
    - The reader side rail buckets `On this page` and `Related pages`; related pages list explicit outgoing links first, then backlinks, and reader/API responses RBAC-filter each item before exposing titles, hrefs, or snippets.
    - Unknown or malformed directives become visible author warnings; they should be fixed in YAML, not hidden with raw HTML.
  </reader_experience>
</doc_reader_extensions>

<options>
  <template_syntax>
    **Variables:** `{{column_name}}`
    **Formatting helpers:**
    | Helper | Example | Output |
    |---|---|---|
    | `{{currency_short val}}` | `{{currency_short revenue_net}}` | $1.2M, $450K |
    | `{{number val}}` | `{{number installs}}` | 1,234,567 |
    | `{{percent val}}` | `{{percent share}}` | 45.2% |

    **Iteration:**
    ```handlebars
    {{#each top_languages}}{{language}} ({{percent share}}){{#unless @last}}, {{/unless}}{{/each}}
    ```

    **Conditionals:**
    ```handlebars
    {{#gt revenue_net 0}}Revenue is positive!{{/gt}}
    {{#gt unprofitable_share 0.2}}⚠️ Warning: above 20%.{{/gt}}
    ```

    **Links:**
    ```handlebars
    {{link "Open report ->" to="/dashboards/product/samples/01-marketing" passFilters=true}}
    ```
    `passFilters=true` forwards current filter state.

    **Source interpolation:** `{{source:key}}` resolves to DuckDB table name.
    **Filter access:** `{{__filters.key}}` reads URL search params.
    **Selection access:** `{{selection.widget_id}}` for cross-widget data.
  </template_syntax>
</options>

<examples>
  <kpi_cards>
    Use section `layout: horizontal` for KPI card rows:

    ```yaml
    sections:
      - id: kpi-cards
        layout: horizontal
        columns: 3
        widgets:
          - id: revenue-card
            type: markdown
            datasource:
              sql: SELECT SUM(revenue_net) AS revenue FROM {{source:daily}}
            template: |
              ### Revenue
              **{{currency_short revenue}}**
            style: { prose: true, prose_size: sm }

          - id: costs-card
            type: markdown
            datasource:
              sql: SELECT SUM(costs) AS costs FROM {{source:daily}}
            template: |
              ### Costs
              **{{currency_short costs}}**
            style: { prose: true, prose_size: sm }

          - id: roi-card
            type: markdown
            datasource:
              sql: |
                SELECT (SUM(revenue_net) - SUM(costs)) / NULLIF(SUM(costs), 0) * 100 AS roi
                FROM {{source:daily}}
            template: |
              ### ROI
              **{{percent roi}}**
            style: { prose: true, prose_size: sm }
    ```
  </kpi_cards>

  <full_example>
    ```yaml
    - id: campaign_summary_header
      type: markdown
      col_span: 12
      datasource:
        sql: |
          WITH base AS (
            SELECT language, SUM(costs) AS spend FROM {{source:total_by_ad}} GROUP BY language
          ),
          totals AS (
            SELECT SUM(spend) AS total_spend FROM base
          ),
          top AS (
            SELECT language, spend FROM base ORDER BY spend DESC LIMIT 3
          )
          SELECT LIST(STRUCT_PACK(
            language := language,
            share := spend / NULLIF((SELECT total_spend FROM totals), 0)
          )) AS top_languages
          FROM top
      template: |
        ## Total by Campaign
        Top languages: {{#each top_languages}}{{language}} ({{percent share}}){{#unless @last}}, {{/unless}}{{/each}}
      style:
        prose: true
        prose_size: base
        custom_css: "border-b pb-4 mb-4"
    ```
  </full_example>
</examples>

<rules>
  <hard_constraints>
    - `datasource` is always inline `{ sql: "..." }` — never a string key
    - SQL runs in DuckDB (DuckDB syntax, not ClickHouse)
    - Use `{{source:key}}` to reference materialized data sources
    - Template receives a single row — aggregate data in SQL
    - Standard Markdown: `**bold**`, `## headings`, lists, GFM tables
    - When linking to another dashboard, use canonical paths (RBAC grants bind to them)
  </hard_constraints>
</rules>

